HOW TO UPDATE MY SCROLLYTELLING STORY
Project: land_appropriation
Live site: https://landappropriation.vercel.app
GitHub repo: https://github.com/kwenniecheng/land_appropriation

-----------------------------------------
BASIC WORKFLOW (every time you make changes)
-----------------------------------------

1. Edit locally
   Open the project in VS Code and make your changes as normal
   (files in src/, assets, etc.)

2. (Optional) Preview changes before publishing
   In terminal, from the project folder, run:

       npm run dev

   Then open http://localhost:5173 in your browser to check
   everything looks right.

3. Save your changes to git
   In terminal, from the project folder, run these three commands
   one at a time:

       git add .
       git commit -m "describe what you changed"
       git push

4. Publish the update live
   Run:

       npx vercel --prod

   This rebuilds your project and updates the live site at
   landappropriation.vercel.app.

-----------------------------------------
MAKE STEP 4 AUTOMATIC (do this once)
-----------------------------------------

If you connect Vercel to your GitHub repo, every "git push" will
automatically trigger a rebuild + redeploy, so you no longer need
to run "npx vercel --prod" manually.

To set this up, run once:

       vercel git connect

After that, your workflow is just:

       git add .
       git commit -m "describe what you changed"
       git push

...and Vercel handles the rest automatically.

-----------------------------------------
REMINDERS / GOTCHAS
-----------------------------------------

- Always run these commands from inside the project folder
  (the one with package.json). Check with:  pwd

- Large files (videos, raw working files, etc.) should NOT be
  committed to git or deployed. Anything in
  static/assets/work files/ is already excluded via .gitignore
  and .vercelignore.

- If a deploy ever fails with an output directory error again,
  check vercel.json - it should contain:

       {
         "outputDirectory": "build"
       }

  (This project uses @sveltejs/adapter-static, which builds to
  a "build" folder rather than the Vercel default of "public".)